{"id":12427,"date":"2026-07-14T21:44:15","date_gmt":"2026-07-15T04:44:15","guid":{"rendered":"https://staging.kosick.com/?p=12427"},"modified":"2026-07-14T21:44:34","modified_gmt":"2026-07-15T04:44:34","slug":"media-buying-process","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https://staging.kosick.com/media-buying-process/","title":{"rendered":"Inside the Media Buying Process: Six Steps From Brief to Optimization"},"content":{"rendered":"\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Most marketing budgets are won or lost long before an ad ever runs. The media buying process is the discipline of turning a budget into attention: deciding where your audience actually is, negotiating the right rates, placing the buy, and then improving it week after week. Done well, it is the difference between spending money and building demand. Done poorly, it is invisible waste. After 40 years buying media for Canadian brands, we have learned the process itself is the product, so here is how it really works.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Before the tactics, it helps to understand why media buying matters at all: a strong buy stretches every dollar, reaches people traditional targeting misses, and compounds over time. If you want the case for investing in it, start there and then come back for the how.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What the media buying process looks like</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The media buying process is not one action; it is six connected stages. Skip one and the whole campaign wobbles. Here is the full arc, from a blank brief to a campaign you are actively sharpening.</p>\n\n\n<style>.kb-image12427_92da64-f6 .kb-image-has-overlay:after{opacity:0.3;}</style>\n<figure class=\"wp-block-kadence-image kb-image12427_92da64-f6 size-large\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"750\" height=\"499\" src=\"/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/media-buying-750x499.jpg\" alt=\"Media Buying Vancouver HVAC TV Radio\" class=\"kb-img wp-image-11304\" srcset=\"/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/media-buying-750x499.jpg 750w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/02/media-buying-360x239.jpg 360w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/02/media-buying-1536x1022.jpg 1536w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/02/media-buying-1140x758.jpg 1140w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/02/media-buying.jpg 1920w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 750px) 100vw, 750px\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Step 1: Strategy and goal setting</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Everything begins with a decision about what success looks like. A brand-awareness push and a lead-generation push buy completely different media, so we pin down the objective, the primary audience, the geography, the budget, and the timeline first. This is also where we set the measurement plan, because a metric you did not define up front is a metric you cannot defend later. The output of this stage is a media brief every later step answers to.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Step 2: Audience and market research</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Next we find out where the audience genuinely spends attention, not where we assume they do. We look at consumption habits across channels, seasonality, and what competitors are already crowding. Good research is what separates real media buying strategies from guesswork, and it is the reason two brands with the same budget can get wildly different results. The deliverable here is a channel shortlist ranked by fit, not by fashion.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Step 3: Planning and channel mix</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">With the research in hand, we build the plan: which channels, in what proportion, at what flight dates, for what share of budget. A modern mix usually blends broadcast reach with digital precision, and knowing how each channel behaves is a core part of what a media buyer does. We model reach and frequency, decide where the money works hardest, and leave room to shift spend once the campaign is live.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Step 4: Negotiation and buying</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">This is the stage people picture when they hear &#8220;media buying,&#8221; and it is where agency relationships earn their keep. We negotiate rates, placements, added value, and make-goods with vendors, then execute the buy. Rate cards are a starting point, not a price, and decades of vendor relationships translate directly into lower costs and better inventory. Whether the buy runs across TV, streaming, and radio or across paid social and search, the goal is the same: more of the right audience for less.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Step 5: Trafficking and launch</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A negotiated buy still has to be delivered correctly. Trafficking is the unglamorous, essential work of getting the right creative, in the right spec, to the right vendor, on the right date, with the right tracking attached. A single mismatched spec or a missing tracking tag can quietly break a campaign, so this stage is all about verification before anything goes live.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Step 6: Optimization and reporting</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The buy is not the finish line; it is the starting line. Once the campaign is running, we watch performance against the goals set in step one and move money toward what is working. Underperforming placements get cut, strong ones get more weight, and the reporting translates raw numbers into decisions. This feedback loop is why an experienced buyer beats a set-and-forget approach every time.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Where most media buying goes wrong</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The two most common failures are skipping the research and stopping at the buy. Skip the research and you buy the wrong channels confidently. Stop at the buy and you leave the biggest gains, the optimization gains, on the table. The media buying process is valuable precisely because it is a loop, not a line: every campaign teaches you something you carry into the next one. If you want to go deeper on a specific channel, our guide to how to buy TV advertising in Canada walks through one medium end to end.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Working with a partner who owns the whole process</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">You can run this process in-house, but each stage rewards specialization: strategists who set the right goal, researchers who find the real audience, buyers with the relationships to negotiate, and analysts who never stop optimizing. Kosick has done all four under one roof for Canadian brands for over 40 years. If you are weighing whether to build that capability internally or bring in a partner, the fastest first step is a conversation about your goals and your budget.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Ready to put a disciplined media buying process behind your next campaign? Get in touch and we will map the first three steps with you.</p>\n\n\n<style>.kb-row-layout-id12427_6ab675-5e > .kt-row-column-wrap{align-content:start;}:where(.kb-row-layout-id12427_6ab675-5e > .kt-row-column-wrap) > .wp-block-kadence-column{justify-content:start;}.kb-row-layout-id12427_6ab675-5e > .kt-row-column-wrap{column-gap:var(--global-kb-gap-md, 2rem);row-gap:var(--global-kb-gap-md, 2rem);max-width:700px;margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto;padding-top:50px;padding-bottom:50px;grid-template-columns:minmax(0, 1fr);}.kb-row-layout-id12427_6ab675-5e > .kt-row-layout-overlay{opacity:0.30;}@media all and (max-width: 1024px){.kb-row-layout-id12427_6ab675-5e > .kt-row-column-wrap{grid-template-columns:minmax(0, 1fr);}}@media all and (max-width: 767px){.kb-row-layout-id12427_6ab675-5e > .kt-row-column-wrap{grid-template-columns:minmax(0, 1fr);}}</style><div class=\"kb-row-layout-wrap kb-row-layout-id12427_6ab675-5e alignfull has-theme-palette4-background-color kt-row-has-bg wp-blo